[X] \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{4{e}^{4}})-2(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2}}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}-1+\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427365 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186427347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427365 #edb/ref 1069 "q-152921" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427365 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427365 #edb/ref 1071 "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{x}^{3}, & 0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}, & 1<x\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{5}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427365 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6e408aa1-cabb-5d40-b79a-58877a0c50b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427365 #edb/ref 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the fifth raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{5}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{5}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{5}\\cdot (1-{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{5}-{x}^{8})\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{6}\\,{x}^{6}-\\frac{1}{9}\\,{x}^{9}){|}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{1}{24}\\,{x}^{6}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{9}-0)+\\frac{1}{24}(64-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}+\\frac{21}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{193}{72}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427366 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186427352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427366 #edb/ref 1069 "q-152923" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427366 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427366 #edb/ref 1071 "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}, & 0\\le x<1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\,x, & 1\\le x\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{4}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427366 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6e9a565a-12f8-5f6b-b609-40f05cb5f2ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427366 #edb/ref 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the fourth raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{4}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{4}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\,{x}^{5}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{1}{12}\\,{x}^{6}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}(1-0)+\\frac{1}{12}(64-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}+\\frac{21}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{53}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427367 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186427357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427367 #edb/ref 1069 "q-90451" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427367 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427367 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{E}[2{X}^{2}+1].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=1-\\frac{x}{4},\\,1<x<3\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427367 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "78882664-5ca1-5d0e-9798-9cca3f283762" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427367 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we simplify using the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[2{X}^{2}+1]=2\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+1\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot (1-\\frac{x}{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}({x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{16}){|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=\\frac{11}{3}$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[2{X}^{2}+1] & =2\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{11}{3}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427368 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186427346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427368 #edb/ref 1069 "q-152899" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427368 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427368 #edb/ref 1071 "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & -1<x<0, \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}\\,x, & 0\\le x<1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427368 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "84598522-637e-5d77-b555-f1f4d414ed22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427368 #edb/ref 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the expected value of ${X}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the expected value of ${X}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\,x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{4}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\,{x}^{5}\\,{|}_{-1}^{0}+\\frac{1}{3}\\,{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(0+1)+\\frac{1}{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427369 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186427355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427369 #edb/ref 1069 "q-90454" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427369 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427369 #edb/ref 1071 "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=4{x}^{3},\\,0<x<1\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{4}{5},$ compute $\\text{E}[9{X}^{2}+X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\text{E}[aX+bY]=a\\text{E}[X]+b\\text{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427369 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8ee689ae-d309-5b9e-a1ff-6e4de3b135ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427369 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[9{X}^{2}+X]=9\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+\\text{E}[X]\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot 4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{2{x}^{6}}{3}\\,|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{4}{5}$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=\\frac{2}{3}$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[9{X}^{2}+X] & =9\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =6+\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{34}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427370 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186427354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427370 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131143" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427370 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427370 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $X$ is a continuous random variable with the following probability density function, compute $\\text{E}[2{X}^{2}-5].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{3},\\,0<x<3\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427370 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "90065876-8b39-51cd-875c-5aa5721dc0eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427370 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we simplify using the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[2{X}^{2}-5]=2\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-5\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9}\\,|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=3$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[2{X}^{2}-5] & =2\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-5 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-5 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427371 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186427350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427371 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131152" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427371 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427371 #edb/ref 1071 "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\,1<x<2\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ compute $\\text{E}[2X-5{X}^{2}].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\text{E}[aX+bY]=a\\text{E}[X]+b\\text{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427371 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "993709fe-5242-5062-93c5-df6c430c3cdf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427371 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[2X-5{X}^{2}]=2\\text{E}[X]-5\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=2$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[2X-5{X}^{2}] & =2\\text{E}[X]-5\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427372 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186427351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427372 #edb/ref 1069 "q-152900" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427372 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427372 #edb/ref 1071 "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{3}\\,{x}^{3}, & 0<x<1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}, & 1\\le x<2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427372 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9e993c85-9e06-5795-8658-efd529bfafbc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427372 #edb/ref 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the expected value of ${X}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the expected value of ${X}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3}\\,{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}\\,{x}^{6}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{1}{9}\\,{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}(1-0)+\\frac{1}{9}(8-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}+\\frac{7}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427373 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186427359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427373 #edb/ref 1069 "q-152893" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427373 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427373 #edb/ref 1071 "A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,1\\le x\\le e.\n$$\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427373 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "da4e5265-9949-5f02-b346-af79bf8d00ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186427373 #edb/ref 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the expected value of ${X}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe compute the expected value of ${X}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
